k about it fur seals king penguins  around antarctica their numbers have  shot up  why it's very simple we've stopped  killing them all these creatures used to  be hunted  for their fur or for their blubber or  something like that so there was you  know there was a massive industry  exploiting these creatures which has  petered out and it's petered out partly  because of pressure from  conservationists but partly also  because the products it produces are no  longer needed because we get them from  fossil fuels instead we don't need whale  blubber or walrus blubber um  it was the discovery of petroleum that  meant that we didn't have to continue  hunting whales for whale oil to the same  extent  and likewise we've all got central  heating because of  fossil fuels and so we don't need  polar bear furs in our in our houses  so my point is simply that  you know when you think about the  recovery of wildlife in areas where it's  doing well  an awful lot of the credit goes to  technology and growth and economic  development  rather some of the credit goes to  conservationists i'm not saying that  they're not important but i am saying  that one uh the things that get blamed  the economic development is actually  part of the solution not part of the  problem  the other story i want to mention is the  island of hispaniola which is divided  between two countries haiti and the  dominican republic on this map haiti is  green and the dominican republic is  yellow that's the wrong way round  actually because if you were to look at  a satellite photograph of those two  countries haiti is brown the dominican  republic is green the border stands out  like a sore thumb on satellite  photographs  why is that  it's because haiti relies on renewable  energy  haiti fuels not just its domestic  economy but its industrial economy using  charcoal basically it's 98 deforested  because everybody's using wood for every  day for the main fuel for the entire  country the dominican republic imports  fossil fuels  and it subsidizes propane as a cooking  fuel so that people won't go out into  the forest and cut down trees  uh to cook over  so in other words it's the it's the  fossil fuels that are sparing  um uh the forest in the dominican river  the dominican republic forest is  increasing the remarkable thing about  fossil fuels when you think about it  is that when we use them  no other animal is deprived of its  livelihood  in other words we're not pinching them  no other animal wants to eat coal or oil  or gas  when but they there you know if you cut  down a tree to burn it you're depriving  a woodpecker of its life or if you kill  a whale you're depriving a killer whale  of its livelihood um  a lot of these renewable energy sources  are actually we're competing with  wildlife for it the great thing about  fossil fuels is you're not competing  with wildlife  which brings me to bio energy  at the moment we're spent we're  diverting about five percent of the  world's grain crop  into motor fuel to run cars instead of  to feed people  that's saving about point six percent of  the world's oil use it's it's cutting  our oil use by about point six percent  which is trivial it's having very little  impact on our use of oil but it's having  an enormous impact on the price of food  as we know fruit prices have doubled in  the last couple of years that's almost  entirely because of this diversion of 40  of america's maize crop for example into  biofuel  and that is driving up hunger and it's  causing starvation there's no question  about it people are being tipped into  malnutrition and even death by this by  this policy  and it's encouraging the destruction of  rainforest because by putting out the  price of food it encourages people to  cut down rainforest and plant  food instead it's happening in and  either to plant fuel like  bio  palm oil in southeast asia or to plant  food instead  so this policy and britain by the way is  is pursuing a policy of of uh trying to  encourage the burning of wood  to produce electricity we're setting up  wood fired power stations all over the  country and at the rate we're going  within five years we will have enough  wood burning capacity that is roughly  five times the total  product the total amount of wood we can  produce in the country so we'll be  importing eighty percent of it quite a  lot some of it's coming from california  already there's wood products being  shipped from california to northern  england to be burned for green energy  a it produces more carbon dioxide than  coal  particularly if you ship it halfway  across the world b it's being pinched  from woodpeckers in california i i  simply don't understand how people think  that this is is good for the planet  so  my argument is simply that the planet  surprisingly is getting greener at the  moment  it's getting greener because of carbon  dioxide because of climate change  because of  the fact that we're needing less land to  produce our food  and that greening  is actually good news and it's not  happening  in spite of what we do in terms of  economic development it's happening  because of economic development thank  you  [Applause]  you
 

